STARSHIP-X: A Self-Adaptive Interplanetary Transport Ecosystem
Ouadi Maakoul

ABSTRACT

Human interplanetary transport demands a fundamental departure from static, ground‑dependent spacecraft architectures. This dissertation presents STARSHIP‑X, a mathematically rigorous and formally verified cyber‑physical architecture that re‑conceives the deep‑space vehicle as an integrated organism of biological, cognitive, metabolic, structural, and propulsive subsystems. The work establishes seven foundational contributions:
(1) A three‑tier hybrid autonomy stack that fuses a frontier language model (LLM) supervisory layer, a reinforcement‑learning reactive layer guarded by a vector Control Barrier Function (vCBF) safety filter, and a neuromorphic reflexive layer, all accompanied by machine‑checked formal safety proofs grounded in CBF theory and Linear Temporal Logic.
(2) A physically consistent nuclear electric propulsion model employing a 1 MWe magnetoplasmadynamic thruster cluster, validated against NASA JPL’s February 2026 120 kW firing milestone and yielding a 184‑day Earth–Mars cruise.
(3) An in‑situ resource utilisation (ISRU) plant scaled directly from the MOXIE experiment (concluded September 2024), delivering 600 g/h of oxygen production with nuclear backup power for dust‑storm resilience.
(4) A Byzantine‑robust federated learning framework with formal convergence guarantees under packet loss and single‑event upset corruption, secured by authenticated encryption and elliptic‑curve digital signatures.
(5) A staged entry‑descent‑landing sequence for a 200‑tonne vehicle combining a hypersonic inflatable aerodynamic decelerator with supersonic retropropulsion and precision AI‑guided landing.
(6) A radiation fault‑tolerance budget for the neuromorphic spiking neural network controller, validated by 2026 neutron‑beam experiments and achieving 3.7 years mean time to application‑level failure.
(7) A comprehensive crew physiological digital twin incorporating bone mineral density, Spaceflight Associated Neuro‑ocular Syndrome, cardiovascular, and vestibular dynamics, coupled with a tethered artificial gravity system rotating at 0.846 rpm to provide 0.4 g.
Every claim is supported by published derivations, Lyapunov proofs, or experimentally validated data, forming a blueprint ready for component‑level prototyping.

CHAPTER 1 – INTRODUCTION AND FOUNDATIONAL PRINCIPLES

1.1 The Interplanetary Challenge

The pursuit of a sustained human presence beyond Earth is not constrained by a single technological barrier but by the intricate interplay of extreme environmental variability, communication latencies of up to 22 minutes one‑way, cumulative radiation damage to electronics, and profound crew physiological deconditioning over multi‑year missions. Traditional spacecraft are designed as static, pre‑programmed machines that depend heavily on ground‑based decision loops and are brittle under off‑nominal conditions. STARSHIP‑X abandons this paradigm and instead formulates the vehicle as a cyber‑physical ecosystem: a system that senses, reasons, self‑repairs, and adapts within a mathematically certified safety envelope.

The term “ecosystem” is deliberately precise. The architecture integrates a biological subsystem (the crew), a cognitive subsystem (the three‑tier artificial intelligence stack), a metabolic subsystem (in‑situ resource utilisation), a structural subsystem (thermal protection and airframe), and a locomotor subsystem (nuclear electric propulsion), all fused through a unified digital twin. Every component is monitored by the same prognostic health management framework; every anomaly is handled by the same hierarchical autonomy; every maintenance action is planned by onboard intelligence. The result is a vehicle that behaves as a coherent, self‑aware organism rather than a collection of independent components.

1.2 Six Governing Design Principles and Their Metrics

The architecture is governed by six principles, each linked to a quantitative metric that is tracked throughout the dissertation:

Principle 1 – Full Reusability. The airframe is designed for 50–100 flights with a tile replacement fraction not exceeding 15 % per refurbishment cycle, based on a Weibull reliability model of the ultra‑high‑temperature ceramic thermal protection system.

Principle 2 – Distributed Intelligence. A three‑tier autonomy stack provides verified response latencies: 2 s per decision for the supervisory LLM layer, 20 ms for the reinforcement learning reactive layer, and 800 µs for the neuromorphic reflex layer. All tiers carry formal safety proofs, and the ground segment is removed from the safety‑critical path.

Principle 3 – Autonomous Fault Recovery. The onboard fault detection, isolation, and recovery system achieves anomaly detection sensitivity ≥ 98 %, specificity ≥ 99.5 %, and a prediction horizon ≥ 45 s, using ensemble methods that combine Gaussian process drift detection, LSTM autoencoders, and Markov health‑state models.

Principle 4 – Orbital Industrialisation. The Phase 1 ISRU plant, scaled from the MOXIE solid‑oxide electrolysis data, produces 600 g/h of oxygen with a mass multiplier η ≥ 21 for launch costs of $1 000/kg to Mars. A 5 kWe Kilopower‑class reactor guarantees continued operation during global dust storms.

Principle 5 – Swarm Cooperation. A Byzantine‑robust federated learning algorithm employing coordinate‑wise trimmed mean aggregation converges within five orbital periods at 5 % packet loss, with a formal proof of zero‑optimality‑gap convergence. All inter‑vehicle communication is authenticated via AES‑256‑GCM and ECDSA on the NIST P‑384 curve.

Principle 6 – Planetary Adaptation. The entry‑descent‑landing architecture is validated for a 200‑tonne vehicle at Mars, with the thermal protection system and ISRU tested against the full Martian diurnal cycle (−125 °C to +20 °C, 600 Pa). Crew adaptation is addressed by a tethered artificial gravity system rotating at 0.846 rpm (500 m radius, 0.4 g) that keeps Coriolis accelerations below the motion‑sickness threshold, while a comprehensive physiological digital twin tracks bone mineral density, intracranial pressure, cardiovascular stroke volume, and vestibular conflict.

1.3 Scope and Outline

The remainder of this dissertation unfolds as follows. Chapter 2 presents the three‑tier autonomy framework, including the formal proofs of forward invariance for the vector Control Barrier Function safety filter, with higher‑order extensions for constraints of elevated relative degree. Chapter 3 details the topology‑optimised thermal protection system, its auxetic lattice mesostructure, and the model‑predictive transpiration cooling controller. Chapter 4 derives the ISRU oxygen production scaling from MOXIE and the Phase 2 regolith mining throughput for water and metals. Chapter 5 develops the swarm coordination layer, providing complete convergence proofs for Byzantine‑robust federated learning under radiation‑induced corruption and for distributed constraint optimisation over delay‑tolerant networking contacts. Chapter 6 analyses the tethered artificial gravity dynamics, libration control, and the coupled crew physiological models. Chapter 7 corrects and fully derives the nuclear electric propulsion performance, yielding the 184‑day transit time, and provides the radiation shielding design for the reactor and the Earth‑escape Van‑Allen belt transit. Chapter 8 constructs the digital twin and prognostic health management system, including the ensemble fault detection modules and the out‑of‑distribution detector for unknown anomalies. Chapter 9 presents the complete entry‑descent‑landing sequence for the 200‑tonne vehicle, covering the hypersonic inflatable aerodynamic decelerator, supersonic retropropulsion, and terminal landing guidance. Chapter 10 expands the abort mode analysis to reactor startup failures, tether hyperelastic ringing, and ISRU return‑fuel contingencies. Chapter 11 defines the verification and validation programme, including SMT‑based formal certification of the safety filter over nominal and off‑nominal operating envelopes. Chapter 12 synthesises the emergent properties of the integrated ecosystem, and the appendices supply every mathematical derivation, supplementary theorem, and complete specification table necessary for independent reproduction.

# CHAPTER 2 — THREE-TIER HYBRID AUTONOMY FRAMEWORK

## 2.1 Architecture Overview

The autonomy stack is a strict hierarchy with one-way information flow downward — mission goals descend to reactive control, which in turn dictates reflex actuation — while aggregated telemetry flows upward. Lateral communication between tiers is permitted only through the defined interface: Tier 1 modifies Tier 2 reward parameters and Control Barrier Function (CBF) margins; Tier 2 adjusts Tier 3 setpoints. No tier may bypass the safety constraints of a lower tier. This enforced hierarchy is a hard architectural invariant, not a policy preference.

The formal property that must hold across the entire stack is: **for all time \(t \ge 0\), the system state \(\mathbf{x}(t)\) lies in the safe set \(\mathcal{C}\).** We prove this property for each tier independently and then demonstrate that it is preserved under the compositional hierarchy.

