The graphene interlayer of earlier design iterations is replaced by a 50 µm hafnium carbide–silicon carbide fibre interface (HfC-SiC). HfC possesses a melting point of 3 958 K and maintains oxidation stability above 2 000 K through the formation of a protective HfO₂-SiO₂ glassy phase. The fracture toughness of this interface layer is \(K_{\text{Ic}} = 4.2\) MPa·m¹/², compared to 2.5 MPa·m¹/² for monolithic ZrB₂, providing effective crack deflection at the ZrB₂/SiC boundary.

## 3.2 Model Predictive Transpiration Cooling

### 3.2.1 Problem Formulation

The transpiration cooling control problem is: given the current extended Kalman filter (EKF) estimate of the thermal state vector \(\hat{\mathbf{T}}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n\), compute the coolant mass flux \(\dot{m}_c''(t)\) over a prediction horizon \(N_p = 300\) steps (30 s at \(\Delta t = 0.1\) s) that minimises total coolant consumption subject to peak temperature constraints.

The Model Predictive Control (MPC) formulation is:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{\dot{m}_c''(k|t)} \quad &\sum_{k=0}^{N_p-1} \bigl[\dot{m}_c''(k|t)^2 \cdot \Delta t\bigr] \\
\text{subject to} \quad &\hat{\mathbf{T}}(k+1|t) = \mathbf{A}_d \hat{\mathbf{T}}(k|t) + \mathbf{B}_{d,q} \, q_{\text{ext}}(k) + \mathbf{B}_{d,c} \, \dot{m}_c''(k|t), \\
&\hat{T}_{\max}(k|t) \le 1\,800\ \text{K} \quad \text{for all } k = 0, \ldots, N_p, \\
&\dot{m}_c''(k|t) \in [0, \dot{m}_{c,\max}''],
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\mathbf{A}_d = e^{\mathbf{A} \Delta t}\) is the discrete-time state transition matrix obtained from the reduced-order model (ROM), \(\mathbf{B}_{d,q}\) and \(\mathbf{B}_{d,c}\) are the discrete-time input matrices for external heat flux and coolant mass flux respectively, and \(\hat{T}_{\max}\) is the maximum temperature over all monitored nodes.

### 3.2.2 QP Structure and Solution

The MPC problem is a strictly convex quadratic programme (QP). Substituting the linear state dynamics forward yields a condensed form:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u}} \quad &\mathbf{u}^T \mathbf{u} \cdot \Delta t \\
\text{subject to} \quad &\mathbf{H} \mathbf{u} \le \mathbf{b}, \\
&\mathbf{0} \le \mathbf{u} \le \dot{m}_{c,\max}'' \mathbf{1},
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\mathbf{u} = [\dot{m}_c''(0), \ldots, \dot{m}_c''(N_p-1)]^T\), and the rows of \(\mathbf{H}\) encode the temperature response at each prediction step to each past control input. The QP has \(n = N_p = 300\) decision variables and \(2N_p = 600\) inequality constraints. It is solved at 10 Hz using a warm-started active-set method embedded on the avionics processor, with computation time under 5 ms.

### 3.2.3 Performance Advantage over PID

The critical advantage of the MPC formulation over classical proportional-integral-derivative (PID) control is its predictive capability. During the Mars aerocapture entry pulse, the external heat flux \(q_{\text{ext}}\) peaks at approximately \(t = 60\) s and then decays. The MPC controller uses the embedded thermal ROM to anticipate this peak 15–20 s in advance, pre-increasing coolant flow before the surface temperature begins to rise. Validated in simulation, this predictive strategy reduces total coolant consumption by 22 % compared to a tuned PID controller while maintaining identical compliance with the 1 800 K temperature constraint.

