With \(c_p \approx 0.84\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}\) for basaltic regolith, \(\Delta T = 300\ \text{K}\), \(L_{\text{fus}} = 334\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}\), and \(L_{\text{vap}} = 2\,501\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}\),

\[
Q_{\text{extraction}} = 33.3 \times 0.84 \times 300 + 1 \times (334 + 2\,501) \approx 8\,392 + 2\,835 = 11\,227\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}.
\]

For a production target of 500 kg sol⁻¹, the continuous thermal power requirement is

\[
P_{\text{thermal}} = \frac{11\,227 \times 500}{86\,400} \approx 65.0\ \text{kW},
\]

which, with 95 % electrical heating efficiency, translates to 68.4 kW electrical. This load is serviced by a dedicated 40 kWe fission reactor augmented by solar‑thermal pre‑heating of the regolith feedstock, bringing the net electrical demand within the reactor allocation.

