## 5.4 DCOP Convergence Under DTN Contact Schedules

The resource allocation problem — distributing fuel, oxygen, and computational tasks among swarm members — is formulated as a Distributed Constraint Optimisation Problem (DCOP). Each agent \(i\) controls a decision variable \(x_i\) (e.g., quantity of fuel to transfer). The factor graph \(\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V} \cup \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{E})\) comprises variable nodes \(\mathcal{V}\) and factor nodes \(\mathcal{F}\) encoding pairwise utility functions \(f_{ij}(x_i, x_j)\).

**Max‑Sum message updates:**

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_{x_i \to f_{ij}}(x_i) &= \sum_{f_{ki} \in \mathcal{F}_i \setminus f_{ij}} \mu_{f_{ki} \to x_i}(x_i), \\
\mu_{f_{ij} \to x_i}(x_i) &= \max_{x_j} \left[ f_{ij}(x_i, x_j) + \mu_{x_j \to f_{ij}}(x_j) \right].
\end{aligned}
\]

On tree‑structured factor graphs, Max‑Sum converges to the globally optimal allocation in exactly two times the depth of the tree in message‑passing rounds. For the Mars swarm, which contains a single cycle in its communication graph, the graph is triangulated and a junction tree is constructed; Max‑Sum executed on this acyclic junction tree retains the convergence guarantee.

**Theorem 5 (DCOP Convergence Under DTN with Packet Loss).** Let the DTN contact schedule define contact windows \(W_1, W_2, \ldots\), each of duration 25 minutes, separated by 100‑minute orbital periods. Let the per‑message loss probability be \(p_{\text{loss}}\). The Max‑Sum algorithm, executed on the junction tree with the time‑expanded factor graph \(\mathcal{G}_T\), converges to a feasible allocation within \(\lceil \log_2(N) \rceil\) contact windows with probability

\[
P_{\text{converge}} \ge \left(1 - p_{\text{loss}}^{M_{\text{per window}}}\right)^{\lceil \log_2(N) \rceil},
\]

where \(M_{\text{per window}}\) is the number of messages exchanged per window. For \(N = 5\), \(M_{\text{per window}} = 20\), and \(p_{\text{loss}} = 0.05\),

\[
P_{\text{converge}} \ge \left(1 - 0.05^{20}\right)^3 \approx 1 - 3 \times 10^{-26} \approx 1.000.
\]

In practice, the binding constraint is message staleness rather than loss: messages older than two orbital periods are discarded, and a local best‑response heuristic serves as a fallback.

