## 5.5 Cybersecurity Architecture

All inter‑vehicle and vehicle‑ground communications are secured at the Bundle Protocol Security (BPSec) layer, which provides integrity and confidentiality services for Bundle Protocol (BPv7) bundles in space delay‑tolerant networks, as defined in IETF RFC 9172 and aligned with CCSDS standards.

**Encryption.** AES‑256 in Galois/Counter Mode (AES‑256‑GCM) provides authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD), simultaneously ensuring confidentiality and integrity. AES‑GCM implementations on space‑grade FPGAs compliant with CCSDS security standards have been demonstrated to outperform prior art in resource efficiency.

**Authentication.** Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) with the NIST P‑384 curve (secp384r1) provides 192‑bit security strength. P‑384 is specified in FIPS 186‑4 and is part of the Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite, widely deployed in protocols including TLS and X.509 public‑key infrastructure. Each spacecraft carries a unique P‑384 key pair generated and certified at launch; all DTN bundles are signed to prevent replay attacks.

**Key establishment.** Quantum‑resistant key exchange is performed pre‑launch using the Module‑Lattice‑Based Key‑Encapsulation Mechanism (ML‑KEM), standardised as FIPS 203 by NIST in August 2024 and formerly known as CRYSTALS‑Kyber. This future‑proofs the communication security against quantum adversaries throughout the mission’s 10‑year operational lifespan.

**Certificate revocation.** A compact Bloom‑filter‑based Certificate Revocation List (CRL) is synchronised across the swarm at each contact window, enabling detection and isolation of a compromised vehicle within one orbital period.

**Federated‑learning poisoning defence.** The cryptographic layer (AES‑256‑GCM + ECDSA) prevents an external adversary from injecting forged model updates into the federated learning pipeline. The CWTM aggregation layer (Section 5.2) tolerates internally corrupted updates resulting from radiation‑induced SEUs. Together, these two layers provide defence‑in‑depth: the cryptographic layer guarantees authenticity and integrity of all messages; the statistical layer guarantees robustness to any residual corruption that escapes the cryptographic perimeter.

