## 6.3 Comprehensive Crew Physiological Digital Twin

The crew health monitoring system integrates four coupled physiological models, each informed by spaceflight data and ground‑based analog studies. The digital twin operates at 0.1 Hz, providing continuous risk assessment to the Tier 1 supervisory autonomy layer.

### 6.3.1 Bone Mineral Density

Prolonged exposure to reduced gravity produces progressive loss of bone mineral density (BMD) through uncoupled bone remodelling — increased osteoclastic resorption and suppressed osteoblastic formation. The BMD dynamics are modelled as a first‑order approach to a gravity‑dependent steady state:

\[
\frac{dB}{dt} = -\frac{1}{\tau_B}\bigl(B - B_{\text{target}}(g)\bigr),
\]

where \(\tau_B\) is the adaptation time constant and \(B_{\text{target}}(g) = B_0 \cdot (g/g_0)^\gamma\) with \(\gamma \approx 0.3\) from bed‑rest analog data. At 0 g, \(\tau_B \approx 200\) days, producing a 180‑day mission loss of approximately 60 % without countermeasures. At 0.4 g, the time constant extends to \(\tau_B \approx 600\) days, reducing the uncountermeasured loss to approximately 26 %.

Exercise countermeasures — specifically, twice‑daily 30‑minute sessions on a vibration‑plate‑equipped Advanced Resistive Exercise Device (ARED) — are modelled as an effective forcing term that increases the time constant to \(\tau_{B,\text{eff}} \approx 2400\) days, limiting the 180‑day transit BMD loss to under 5 %. Exercise countermeasures have not completely eliminated bone loss from long‑duration spaceflight missions, which leaves astronauts susceptible to early‑onset osteoporosis; artificial gravity provides a comprehensive countermeasure capable of challenging multiple physiological systems simultaneously. 

### 6.3.2 Spaceflight‑Associated Neuro‑ocular Syndrome

Spaceflight‑Associated Neuro‑ocular Syndrome (SANS) is a complex collection of ocular changes observed in approximately two‑thirds of astronauts during and after long‑duration space missions. Clinical findings include globe flattening, choroidal folds, optic disc oedema, and hyperopic refractive shifts.  The pathophysiology is multifactorial: cephalad fluid shift elevating intracranial pressure (ICP), altered cerebrospinal fluid dynamics, vascular dysregulation, mitochondrial dysfunction, and radiation exposure all contribute.

**ICP compartment model.** The intracranial pressure is modelled as a two‑compartment system comprising cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and vascular contributions:

\[
C_{\text{CSF}} \frac{d(\text{ICP})}{dt} = Q_{\text{formation}} - Q_{\text{absorption}}(\text{ICP}) + Q_{\text{arterial}}(P_{\text{arterial}}, \mathbf{g}),
\]

where \(Q_{\text{formation}} \approx 0.35\) mL min⁻¹ is the constant CSF production rate, \(Q_{\text{absorption}} = (\text{ICP} - \text{ICP}_{\text{ref}})/R_{\text{arachnoid}}\) is the pressure‑dependent drainage through arachnoid granulations (Starling resistor model), and \(Q_{\text{arterial}}\) captures the gravity‑dependent arterial pulsation component.

In microgravity, the loss of hydrostatic gradient produces a cephalad fluid shift that increases cranial arterial pressure by approximately 20 mmHg, raising ICP by 10–15 mmHg above Earth‑normal. At 0.4 g artificial gravity, the model predicts an ICP elevation of only 3–5 mmHg, well below the optic disc oedema threshold of 15–20 mmHg. Artificial gravity would lessen the cephalad fluid shifts encountered in microgravity, allowing for physiological fluid distribution closer to that on the ground, and has been proposed as a countermeasure for SANS. 

**Limitation.** SANS aetiology extends beyond ICP elevation alone. Globe flattening, choroidal inflammation, and retinal nerve‑fibre‑layer thickening may proceed through ICP‑independent pathways. The ICP model therefore captures approximately 40–50 % of the total SANS risk. Full mitigation requires in‑flight monitoring via fundoscopy or optical coherence tomography (OCT) during the first month of rotation; should disc oedema appear despite normal ICP, the rotation rate is increased to 0.5 g (\(\omega = 0.99\) rpm) as an escalated countermeasure.

### 6.3.3 Cardiovascular Dynamics

Stroke volume (SV) deconditioning follows a gravity‑dependent exponential:

\[
\frac{d(\text{SV})}{dt} = -\frac{1}{\tau_{\text{CV}}}\bigl(\text{SV} - \text{SV}_{\text{ref}}(g)\bigr) + \Delta\text{SV}_{\text{exercise}} \cdot \delta_{\text{exercise}}(t),
\]

with \(\tau_{\text{CV}} \approx 14\) days at 0 g and \(\text{SV}_{\text{ref}}(g) = \text{SV}_{\text{ref}}(1g) \times (g/g_0)^{0.3}\). At 0.4 g, \(\text{SV}_{\text{ref}} \approx 52\) mL. A daily 2‑hour exercise protocol combining ARED resistance training with treadmill running under artificial gravity maintains SV above 50 mL throughout the transit — a level sufficient to prevent orthostatic intolerance upon Mars arrival.

### 6.3.4 Vestibular Adaptation

A particle filter over the vestibulo‑ocular reflex (VOR) state estimates the individual motion‑sickness probability at each time step. The filter state is the tilt‑translation conflict angle \(\phi_{\text{VOR}}\), updated from IMU head‑motion data and the known rotation vector \(\boldsymbol{\omega}\). The MSI probability, as presented in Section 6.1.1, remains below 5 % during the 72‑hour adaptation window and decays to negligible levels thereafter.

