# CHAPTER 7 — NUCLEAR ELECTRIC PROPULSION

## 7.1 Propulsion Architecture

The STARSHIP‑X crewed vehicle employs a 1 MWe fast‑spectrum liquid‑metal‑cooled fission reactor powering a cluster of four 250 kW lithium‑fed magnetoplasmadynamic (MPD) thrusters. This configuration represents the high‑power electric propulsion pathway identified by NASA’s Space Nuclear Propulsion project, which has supported megawatt‑class nuclear electric propulsion (NEP) development for human Mars missions since 2020. 

The MPD thruster accelerates propellant using combined electrostatic and electromagnetic (Lorentz) forces. A high‑current discharge ionises the lithium propellant; the resulting plasma is accelerated by the self‑induced magnetic field, producing thrust without the need for applied magnetic fields or accelerating grids. MPD thrusters have been researched since the 1960s and offer the unique combination of high power density, propellant flexibility, and specific impulse in the 1500–5000 s range. 

### 7.1.1 The JPL 120 kW Milestone

On 24 February 2026, a team at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory ignited a lithium‑fed MPD thruster in the Condensable Metal Propellant (CoMeT) vacuum facility, achieving power levels of up to 120 kW — the highest power level ever attained by a U.S. electric propulsion system and more than 25 times the power of the Hall‑effect thrusters currently operating on NASA’s Psyche mission. During the test, the tungsten electrode at the thruster centre reached temperatures exceeding 2 800 °C. NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman stated: “The successful performance of our thruster in this test demonstrates real progress toward sending an American astronaut to set foot on the Red Planet.” 

The team aims to reach power levels between 500 kW and 1 MW per thruster in the coming years. A human Mars mission is projected to require 2–4 MW of total electric propulsion power, necessitating multiple MPD thrusters operating for more than 23 000 hours.  The STARSHIP‑X cluster of four 250 kW thrusters at 1 MWe total represents a scaling factor of approximately 8× above the 2026 demonstration — an ambitious but credible target for 2038 flight hardware.

---

## 7.2 Performance Derivation

### 7.2.1 Thrust and Specific Impulse

The thrust produced by an electric propulsion system is

\[
T = \frac{2\eta P}{g_0 I_{\text{sp}}},
\]

where \(\eta\) is the thruster efficiency, \(P\) is the input electrical power, \(g_0 = 9.81\) m s⁻² is standard gravity, and \(I_{\text{sp}}\) is the specific impulse. For lithium‑fed MPD thrusters, published performance data report efficiencies of \(\eta = 0.69\) at approximately 5 000 s specific impulse using lithium propellant; conservative designs operating at \(I_{\text{sp}} = 3\,500\) s with \(\eta = 0.65\) provide a balanced trade between thrust density and propellant economy. 

For the STARSHIP‑X cluster at \(P = 1\,000\,000\) W:

\[
T = \frac{2 \times 0.65 \times 10^6}{9.81 \times 3\,500} = \frac{1.30 \times 10^6}{34\,335} \approx 37.9\ \text{N}.
\]

### 7.2.2 Earth–Mars Transit Time

The vehicle initial mass at Earth departure is \(m_0 = 200\,000\) kg, including 18 710 kg of lithium propellant for the outbound leg. For a continuous‑thrust trajectory approximating a logarithmic spiral, the required \(\Delta v\) for Earth–Mars transfer is approximately 3 000 m s⁻¹. The Tsiolkovsky rocket equation gives the final mass:

\[
m_f = m_0 \exp\!\left(-\frac{\Delta v}{g_0 I_{\text{sp}}}\right) = 200\,000 \times \exp\!\left(-\frac{3\,000}{9.81 \times 3\,500}\right) \approx 181\,290\ \text{kg}.
\]

Propellant consumed: \(m_p = m_0 - m_f = 18\,710\) kg, consistent with the outbound xenon‑equivalent budget.

The average acceleration over the burn, using the mean mass \(\bar{m} = (m_0 + m_f)/2 = 190\,645\) kg, is

\[
\bar{a} = \frac{T}{\bar{m}} = \frac{37.9}{190\,645} \approx 1.99 \times 10^{-4}\ \text{m s}^{-2}.
\]

The burn duration to accumulate \(\Delta v = 3\,000\) m s⁻¹ is

\[
t_{\text{burn}} = \frac{\Delta v}{\bar{a}} = \frac{3\,000}{1.99 \times 10^{-4}} \approx 1.51 \times 10^7\ \text{s} \approx 174\ \text{days}.
\]

Adding coast phases, gravitational perturbations, and a Mars orbit insertion margin, the total one‑way Earth–Mars transit is **184 days (approximately 6 months)**. The full trajectory optimisation using Pontryagin’s minimum principle and primer‑vector steering is detailed in Appendix G.

