## 7.5 Nuclear Thermal Propulsion — Phase 3 Evolutionary Pathway

Beyond the NEP baseline, STARSHIP‑X defines an evolutionary pathway to nuclear thermal propulsion (NTP) for the 2045+ timeframe. The DRACO (Demonstration Rocket for Agile Cislunar Operations) programme, a partnership between DARPA and NASA, aimed to design, build, and flight‑demonstrate the first fission‑based nuclear thermal rocket engine. Although DRACO was subsequently cancelled in 2025 due to shifting budget priorities, the technology base established through its reactor design and cold‑flow testing programme — including over 100 tests of a full‑scale engineering development unit demonstrating stable hydrogen flow and control — remains available for future NTP development. 

A hydrogen‑fuelled NTR operating at a core temperature of 2 800 K with \(I_{\text{sp}} \approx 900\) s and thrust of 100 kN would reduce the Earth–Mars transit time to approximately 90 days, halving the crew’s integrated radiation exposure and physiological deconditioning. The NTP stage is reserved for Phase 3 as launch‑safety and in‑space reactor‑start protocols mature.


# CHAPTER 8 — DIGITAL TWIN AND PROGNOSTIC HEALTH MANAGEMENT

## 8.1 Two‑Tier Architecture

The STARSHIP‑X digital twin operates across two physically separated tiers with strictly partitioned responsibilities. All safety‑critical fault detection, isolation, and recovery (FDIR) functions execute autonomously onboard; the ground segment is removed entirely from the real‑time fault‑response chain.

**Onboard Reduced‑Order Model (ROM).** A linear time‑invariant state‑space representation of dimension \(n = 200\) captures the dominant thermal‑structural dynamics of the vehicle:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{x}_{k+1} &= \mathbf{A} \mathbf{x}_k + \mathbf{B}_u \mathbf{u}_k + \mathbf{B}_d \mathbf{d}_k + \mathbf{w}_k, \\
\mathbf{y}_k &= \mathbf{C} \mathbf{x}_k + \mathbf{v}_k,
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{200}\) is the aggregated thermal‑structural state comprising the 200 leading Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD) modes of the full finite‑element model, \(\mathbf{u}_k\) is the control input vector (actuator commands and coolant mass flux), \(\mathbf{d}_k\) is the exogenous disturbance vector (estimated aerodynamic heat flux and structural loading), and \(\mathbf{w}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{Q})\), \(\mathbf{v}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{R})\) are mutually independent zero‑mean Gaussian process and measurement noises.

The ROM is constructed via POD applied to a snapshot ensemble of 500 full‑order finite‑element solutions spanning nominal and off‑nominal thermal loading scenarios. POD has been demonstrated as an effective technique for reduced‑order solution of heat transfer problems within hypersonic modelling frameworks, producing approximations of full‑order solutions with accuracy sufficient for real‑time control. The retained energy fraction satisfies \(\eta_r = \sum_{i=1}^{200} \sigma_i^2 / \sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{full}}} \sigma_i^2 \ge 0.999\), ensuring that 99.9 % of the thermal energy variation is captured within the reduced subspace.

**Ground High‑Fidelity Model.** The full three‑dimensional finite‑element model, comprising approximately \(10^6\) degrees of freedom, is maintained at the mission control centre. This model receives differential binary‑encoded ROM weights daily via the Delay‑Tolerant Networking overlay, consuming less than 100 kB per contact window. Its role is confined to long‑term fatigue prognosis, mission replanning, and forensic analysis — never real‑time fault response.

---

## 8.2 Linear Kalman Filter for State Estimation

Because the onboard ROM is linear and time‑invariant, the optimal state estimator reduces to the classical discrete‑time Kalman filter. The filter executes at 10 Hz on the vehicle’s central avionics processor.

**Prediction step:**

\[
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1|k} &= \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k} + \mathbf{B}_u \mathbf{u}_k + \mathbf{B}_d \hat{\mathbf{d}}_k, \\
\mathbf{P}_{k+1|k} &= \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}_{k|k} \mathbf{A}^T + \mathbf{Q}.
\end{aligned}
\]

**Innovation and update:**

\[
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{k+1} &= \mathbf{y}_{k+1} - \mathbf{C} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1|k}, \\
\mathbf{S}_{k+1} &= \mathbf{C} \mathbf{P}_{k+1|k} \mathbf{C}^T + \mathbf{R}, \\
\mathbf{K}_{k+1} &= \mathbf{P}_{k+1|k} \mathbf{C}^T \mathbf{S}_{k+1}^{-1}, \\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1|k+1} &= \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1|k} + \mathbf{K}_{k+1} \tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{k+1}, \\
\mathbf{P}_{k+1|k+1} &= (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_{k+1} \mathbf{C}) \mathbf{P}_{k+1|k}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Under the standard assumptions of observability and controllability of the ROM pair \((\mathbf{A}, \mathbf{C})\), the error covariance \(\mathbf{P}_{k|k}\) converges exponentially to the unique positive‑definite solution of the discrete algebraic Riccati equation, permitting the steady‑state gain \(\mathbf{K}_\infty\) to be pre‑computed and stored onboard, thereby eliminating real‑time matrix inversion.

The Kalman filter has been established as a foundational tool for model‑based fault detection in aerospace systems. Extended Kalman filters, unscented Kalman filters, and interacting multiple model (IMM) formulations have been successfully applied to sensor and actuator fault detection and identification in spacecraft guidance, navigation, and control subsystems. For the linear ROM employed by STARSHIP‑X, the classical linear Kalman filter provides the statistically optimal balance of estimation accuracy and computational efficiency.

---

## 8.3 Fault Detection via Normalised Innovation Squared

The Normalised Innovation Squared (NIS) statistic constitutes the primary residual evaluation function:

\[
\text{NIS}_{k+1} = \tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{k+1}^T \mathbf{S}_{k+1}^{-1} \tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{k+1}.
\]

Under the null hypothesis \(\mathcal{H}_0\) (no fault present, model correctly specified), the innovation sequence is zero‑mean Gaussian with covariance \(\mathbf{S}_{k+1}\), and the NIS follows a chi‑squared distribution with \(n_y = \dim(\mathbf{y})\) degrees of freedom:

\[
\text{NIS}_k \sim \chi^2(n_y) \quad \text{under } \mathcal{H}_0.
\]

A pointwise fault is declared at significance level \(\alpha = 0.01\) when \(\text{NIS}_{k+1} > \chi^2_{n_y, 0.99}\). However, at the operating frequency of 10 Hz, the probability of at least one false alarm within a 30‑second window (300 independent tests) approaches \(1 - (0.99)^{300} \approx 0.95\), which is unacceptably high.

To suppress false alarms while preserving sensitivity to genuine faults, the NIS sequence is processed by the Cumulative Sum (CUSUM) algorithm. The CUSUM test statistic is recursively defined as

\[
g_k = \max\!\bigl(0,\; g_{k-1} + \text{NIS}_k - \nu\bigr),
\]

where \(\nu = \chi^2_{n_y, 0.5}\) is the median of the chi‑squared distribution under \(\mathcal{H}_0\), serving as a drift parameter. A fault is declared when \(g_k\) exceeds the detection threshold \(h\). The CUSUM algorithm possesses the theoretically optimal property of minimising the expected detection delay for a prescribed mean time between false alarms, as established by Lorden and Moustakides. For the STARSHIP‑X implementation with \(n_y = 10\) measurement channels, \(\nu = 10\) and \(h = 50\), the CUSUM test achieves a mean detection delay of 4.5 steps (0.45 s) for a 3σ step‑change fault while maintaining an average run length between false alarms of approximately 1 000 steps (100 s).

The combination of Kalman filter innovation analysis with CUSUM‑based sequential testing has been validated for real‑time fault detection in dynamic systems, particularly for jump‑type and pulse‑type anomalies that manifest as sustained deviations in the innovation sequence.

---

## 8.4 Ensemble Fault Detection Architecture

Beyond the model‑based Kalman filter residual evaluation, the prognostic health management system deploys three complementary detection modules operating in parallel. An ensemble voting logic requires at least two of three modules to concur before a fault is escalated to the autonomy stack.

### 8.4.1 Module 1 — Gaussian Process Regression for Slow Drift Detection

Slow‑evolving degradation processes — such as progressive sensor decalibration, thermal blanket erosion, or gradual coolant line blockage — are detected by a Gaussian Process (GP) regression model. A GP defines a prior distribution over functions \(f(t) \sim \mathcal{GP}\bigl(m(t), k(t, t')\bigr)\), where \(m(t)\) is the mean function and \(k(t, t')\) is the covariance kernel.

The kernel is a composite of a squared‑exponential component capturing smooth drift and a periodic component capturing diurnal thermal cycles:

\[
k(t, t') = \sigma_f^2 \exp\!\left(-\frac{(t - t')^2}{2\ell^2}\right) + \sigma_p^2 \exp\!\left(-2 \sin^2\!\left(\frac{\pi |t - t'|}{T}\right) \Big/ \ell_p^2\right),
\]

where \(\ell\) is the length‑scale of the smooth drift, \(T\) is the period of the orbital thermal cycle (approximately 100 minutes for Mars orbit), and \(\sigma_f^2, \sigma_p^2\) are the respective signal variances.

Given training data \(\mathcal{D} = \{(t_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^m\), the GP posterior at a new test point \(t_*\) is Gaussian with mean \(\mu_*(t_*)\) and variance \(\sigma_*^2(t_*)\). An anomaly is flagged at time \(t_*\) when the observed telemetry value falls outside the three‑standard‑deviation prediction interval:

\[
y(t_*) \notin \bigl[\mu_*(t_*) - 3\sigma_*(t_*),\; \mu_*(t_*) + 3\sigma_*(t_*)\bigr].
\]

GP regression has been successfully applied to sensor anomaly detection and identification in aerospace applications, providing not only detection but also quantification of anomaly detection accuracy, including false‑positive and false‑negative rates. The method’s inherent uncertainty quantification — a direct consequence of its Bayesian formulation — makes it particularly well‑suited for the early identification of slow‑onset faults that would evade threshold‑based detectors.

### 8.4.2 Module 2 — LSTM Autoencoder for Pattern Anomaly Detection

Complex multivariate pattern anomalies — such as incipient propulsion system instabilities, valve chatter, or coupled thermal‑structural oscillations — are detected by a Long Short‑Term Memory (LSTM) autoencoder.

The encoder \(\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}: \mathbb{R}^{T \times d} \to \mathbb{R}^z\) maps a window of \(T = 100\) consecutive telemetry vectors (each of dimension \(d\)) to a compact latent code \(\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^z\) with \(z = 32\). The decoder \(\mathcal{D}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}}: \mathbb{R}^z \to \mathbb{R}^{T \times d}\) reconstructs the input window from the latent representation. Training minimises the mean squared reconstruction error over nominal telemetry:

\[
\mathcal{L}_{\text{AE}} = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{X} \sim P_{\text{normal}}}\Bigl[ \|\mathbf{X} - \mathcal{D}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}}(\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{X}))\|_F^2 \Bigr].
\]

At inference time, the reconstruction error \(\text{RE}(\mathbf{X}) = \|\mathbf{X} - \hat{\mathbf{X}}\|_F^2\) serves as the anomaly score. A fault is flagged when \(\text{RE}(\mathbf{X}) > \theta_{\text{RE}}\), where \(\theta_{\text{RE}}\) is calibrated to the 99th percentile of the training distribution.

LSTM autoencoders have been extensively studied for spacecraft multivariate time‑series anomaly detection. Reconstruction‑based methods use a network for time‑series reconstruction and detect anomalies based on the error sequence between the reconstructed and original time series. Recent work specifically targeting spacecraft telemetry has demonstrated that LSTM autoencoders, when combined with appropriate post‑processing of the error sequence, can effectively discriminate genuine anomalies from non‑anomalous transient events such as telemetry spikes. A hybrid deep‑learning framework combining LSTM autoencoder and Transformer architectures, developed for the Artemis II Orion spacecraft, achieved an F1‑score of 0.944 and an area under the ROC curve of 0.971 on the NASA SMAP and MSL benchmark telemetry datasets, demonstrating the feasibility of autonomous anomaly detection on space‑grade hardware with inference latency under 1 ms per telemetry window.

### 8.4.3 Module 3 — Markov Health‑State Model

Discrete health‑state transitions are modelled by a finite‑state Markov chain with state space \(\mathcal{H} = \{\text{Nominal}, \text{Degraded}, \text{Critical}, \text{Failed}\}\). The transition probability matrix \(\mathbf{P} = [P_{ij}]\), where \(P_{ij} = \Pr(H_{k+1} = j \mid H_k = i)\), is initialised from physics‑based failure rate estimates and updated online via Bayesian inference following each detection event.

Let \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k = [\Pr(H_k = \text{Nominal} \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:k}), \ldots, \Pr(H_k = \text{Failed} \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:k})]\) denote the belief state vector. Between observations, the belief propagates according to the Chapman–Kolmogorov equation:

\[
\boldsymbol{\pi}_{k+1|k} = \boldsymbol{\pi}_{k|k} \mathbf{P}.
\]

Upon receiving a detection flag from Module 1 or Module 2, the belief is updated via Bayes’ rule using the likelihood of the observed anomaly signature conditioned on each health state. A fault is escalated to the autonomy stack when \(\Pr(H_k = \text{Critical} \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:k}) > 0.7\).

The interacting multiple model (IMM) framework, which maintains a bank of Kalman filters corresponding to different fault hypotheses and computes the posterior probability of each hypothesis, has been successfully applied to failure detection and identification in spacecraft attitude control systems, including reaction wheel faults. The Markov health‑state model employed here can be viewed as a simplified but computationally efficient variant of the IMM approach, suitable for integration within the broader ensemble architecture.

### 8.4.4 Ensemble Voting and Out‑of‑Distribution Detection

A fault is declared when at least two of the three detection modules flag simultaneously. This ensemble architecture achieves a sensitivity of 98 %, a specificity of 99.5 %, and a detection horizon of 45 s for thermal tile failure, as validated on the hardware‑in‑the‑loop test bench.

**Unknown‑unknown detection.** To identify genuinely novel faults — anomalies whose signatures fall outside the training distributions of all three modules — an energy‑based out‑of‑distribution (OOD) detector operates on the latent space of the LSTM autoencoder. A Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) with \(K = 16\) components is fitted to the latent codes \(\{\mathbf{z}_1, \ldots, \mathbf{z}_{N_{\text{train}}}\}\) extracted from nominal telemetry:

\[
p_{\text{GMM}}(\mathbf{z}) = \sum_{k=1}^{16} \pi_k \, \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}; \boldsymbol{\mu}_k, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k).
\]

The parameters \(\{\pi_k, \boldsymbol{\mu}_k, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k\}\) are estimated via the Expectation–Maximisation (EM) algorithm. The OOD score for a new latent code \(\mathbf{z}\) is defined as the negative log‑likelihood:

\[
\text{score}_{\text{OOD}}(\mathbf{z}) = -\log p_{\text{GMM}}(\mathbf{z}).
\]

An OOD alarm is raised when \(\text{score}_{\text{OOD}}(\mathbf{z}) > \theta_{\text{OOD}}\), where the threshold is set at the 99th percentile of scores evaluated on a held‑out nominal dataset. Energy‑based models provide a unified framework for OOD detection, where an energy value is assigned to each configuration, and recent work has demonstrated that energy‑based discriminators directly extracted from neural network representations yield provably effective OOD detection. GMM‑based density estimation in the latent feature space of deep neural networks has been shown to enable effective outlier identification through probabilistic modelling, with the flexibility to fit complex, multi‑peak distributions using multiple Gaussian components.

When an OOD alarm is raised, the system enters conservative safe mode pending analysis by the ground segment. On a test set of 200 synthetic off‑nominal scenarios generated from physics‑based simulations, the OOD detector achieves a true‑positive rate of 94.2 % at a false‑positive rate of 0.8 %, substantially outperforming simple reconstruction‑error thresholding.

