## 8.5 Digital Thread Integration

The digital twin serves as the vehicle’s nervous system, connecting every subsystem through a unified state representation:

- The Kalman filter thermal state estimate \(\hat{\mathbf{T}}\) feeds the Model Predictive Control (MPC) transpiration cooling controller (Chapter 3).
- The MPC coolant mass flux command \(\dot{m}_c''\) feeds back into the Kalman filter as a control input \(\mathbf{u}_k\).
- The LSTM autoencoder monitors the Kalman innovation sequence for anomalies that the linear filter cannot capture, such as sensor drift or incipient nonlinear instabilities.
- The Tier 2 vector Control Barrier Function (vCBF) safety filter (Chapter 2) incorporates the thermal state via barrier \(h_4(\mathbf{x}) = T_{\text{allow}} - T_{\max}(\mathbf{x})\).
- The Tier 1 LLM playbook (Chapter 2) includes a rule: “If thermal anomaly probability exceeds 0.3, increase orbit altitude by 5 km to reduce aerodynamic heating.”
- The crew physiological model (Chapter 6) provides health risk vectors that modify Tier 1 mission priorities.
- The propulsion health model provides effective specific impulse estimates to the trajectory optimiser.

This closed digital thread operates at four distinct timescales — 800 µs (Tier 3 neuromorphic reflexes), 20 ms (Tier 2 reactive control), 2 s (Tier 1 supervisory reasoning), and 24 h (ground model synchronisation) — realising the ecosystem metaphor at the information‑architectural level.

