### 11.2.2 Key Test Events

**VV‑01 — Tier 3 SNN Neutron‑Beam Radiation Testing.** The neuromorphic SNN controller, fabricated on the Frontgrade Gaisler space‑grade processor with BrainChip Akida IP, is exposed to a high‑energy neutron beam at the Los Alamos Neutron Science Center (LANSCE). The neutron spectrum is tuned to replicate the galactic cosmic ray secondary neutron environment at 1 AU. The acceptance criterion is a mean time to application‑level failure exceeding 3 years, as validated by the hierarchical temporal defence (HTD) architecture (Section 2.4.2). The test methodology follows that developed for neuromorphic processors with on‑chip synaptic plasticity, which has been experimentally validated by mapping SNN processors onto flash‑based FPGAs and exposing them to high‑energy neutron beams.

**VV‑02 — vCBF Safety Filter Formal Verification.** The SMT‑based verification described in Section 11.1 constitutes the formal certification evidence for the safety filter. The verification report, including the dReal proof logs, the sampling methodology, and the off‑nominal scenario coverage analysis, is submitted as part of the software safety case under the framework of RTCA DO‑178C (Software Considerations in Airborne Systems and Equipment Certification) and DO‑326A (Airworthiness Security Process Specification). DO‑326A establishes the Airworthiness Security Process for civil aviation and provides guidance for handling the threat of intentional, malicious interference with aircraft systems, complementing the hardware and software safety certification guidance documents DO‑254 and DO‑178C.

**VV‑03 — GUIDE Playbook Verification.** The GUIDE in‑context learning framework is verified via Monte Carlo simulation over \(10^5\) synthetic mission episodes. The acceptance criterion is that the playbook policy exhibits monotonic improvement: \(\mathbb{E}[r_{t+1}] \ge \mathbb{E}[r_t]\) for all \(t\), consistent with the theoretical result established in the GUIDE framework. Each episode simulates a full Earth–Mars cruise with stochastic fault injection (thruster degradation, sensor drift, thermal anomalies), and the playbook’s action recommendations are compared against a ground‑truth optimal policy computed offline.

**VV‑04 — ZrB₂-SiC UHTC Tile Arc‑Jet Testing.** Full‑scale ZrB₂-SiC ultra‑high‑temperature ceramic tiles with the HfC‑SiC interface layer are exposed to 20 thermal cycles at 2 200 K in the NASA Ames Arc‑Jet Complex. The acceptance criterion is that the Weibull shape parameter \(\beta \ge 2.5\) and the characteristic life \(\eta \ge 170\) cycles at 90 % confidence, consistent with the reliability model presented in Section 3.1.4.

**VV‑05 — MPC Transpiration Cooling Hardware‑in‑the‑Loop.** The model predictive transpiration cooling controller (Section 3.2) is tested in a hardware‑in‑the‑loop configuration: the reduced‑order thermal model runs in real time on the flight‑representative avionics processor, driving a physical coolant flow control valve, while a high‑fidelity aerothermal simulator injects synthetic heat‑flux profiles corresponding to the Mars entry trajectory. The acceptance criterion is that the maximum surface temperature never exceeds 1 800 K across 500 Monte Carlo entry profiles.

**VV‑06 — ISRU Phase 1 Subscale Plant Mars Chamber Test.** A subscale solid‑oxide electrolysis plant (10 kg, 100 W) derived from the MOXIE flight design is operated continuously for 1 000 hours in a Mars environmental chamber at 600 Pa and −60 °C to +20 °C diurnal cycling. MOXIE ended its successful mission on 30 September 2024, having satisfied all its success criteria and achieving twice its required oxygen production rate, and suffering no failures over the course of its 16 operational runs on Mars. The acceptance criterion for the scaled plant is an oxygen production rate of at least 400 g h⁻¹ at 98 % purity, corresponding to the conservative operational rating adopted in Section 4.1.

**VV‑07 — HIAD Deployment Balloon Drop Test.** A 10‑metre‑diameter subscale HIAD is deployed from a high‑altitude balloon at 35 km altitude. The Low‑Earth Orbit Flight Test of an Inflatable Decelerator (LOFTID) mission, conducted on 10 November 2022, demonstrated a 6‑metre HIAD that endured the harsh environments of atmospheric re‑entry while exhibiting stable aerodynamics through the entire spectrum of hypersonic, supersonic, transonic, and subsonic flight. The STARSHIP‑X subscale test extends this heritage to a larger diameter and validates the inflation dynamics model (Appendix L) for the 20 m full‑scale HIAD.

**VV‑08 — Byzantine‑Robust Federated Learning Simulation.** A 5‑vehicle federated learning simulation with one Byzantine node (injecting arbitrary gradient perturbations) exercises the coordinate‑wise trimmed mean aggregation algorithm. The acceptance criterion is convergence to within 1 % of the optimal thruster‑degradation model within 25 orbital periods at 5 % packet loss, consistent with Theorem 3 (Section 5.2.2).

**VV‑09 — 3‑Vehicle LEO Formation Control Flight Test.** Three CubeSats (6U form factor) equipped with cold‑gas thrusters and UHF inter‑satellite links execute the passivity‑based formation control law (Section 5.3) in low Earth orbit. The acceptance criterion is a steady‑state formation error below 0.5 m after 5 minutes of manoeuvring. This test also validates the communication‑blackout protocol (Section 5.3.1) by commanding simulated occultations.

**VV‑10 — SRP Throttling Altitude Drop Test.** A subscale Raptor‑X engine is test‑fired in a supersonic wind tunnel at Mach 3.5 to validate the plume‑freestream interaction model and the plume efficiency factor \(\eta_{\text{plume}}(M)\). Supersonic retropropulsion has been the subject of a multi‑year NASA research programme involving CFD analysis, wind‑tunnel testing, and flight data reconstruction in partnership with SpaceX. The STARSHIP‑X test extends this heritage to the methalox propellant combination and the Raptor‑X engine cycle.

**VV‑11 — ISS‑Based Subscale Tether Deployment.** A 100‑metre subscale tether is deployed from the International Space Station to validate the deployment dynamics, libration damping control law, and load‑cell tension monitoring system. The acceptance criterion is that the libration angle is damped to below 1° within two orbital periods.

**VV‑12 — Full Uncrewed EDL Demonstrator at Mars.** A ½‑scale (100 t) STARSHIP‑X vehicle, equipped with the full HIAD + SRP EDL sequence, is launched to Mars during the 2033 opportunity. The vehicle executes the complete entry, descent, and landing sequence autonomously. The acceptance criterion is a successful landing within 50 m of the targeted location, with all telemetry confirming that thermal, structural, and propellant margins remained within the predicted envelopes.

**VV‑13 — Full Uncrewed Mars Orbital Mission.** Two identical STARSHIP‑X vehicles, without crew, execute the complete Earth–Mars NEP spiral, Mars orbit insertion, and Earth return. These missions validate the integrated propulsion, autonomy, digital twin, and communication architectures over the full mission duration.

