## 13.3 Limitations and Future Work

The STARSHIP‑X architecture, while mathematically rigorous and physically grounded, is not without limitations. These limitations define the agenda for future research.

### 13.3.1 Technology Readiness Advancement

Several subsystems currently reside at TRL 3–5 and require substantial investment to reach flight qualification (TRL 8–9). The 1 MWe MPD thruster cluster represents an eight‑fold scaling above the 120 kW JPL demonstration. The 20‑metre HIAD must be manufactured, folded, and deployed at a scale three times larger than the LOFTID flight article. The Kilopower reactor must be uprated from the 1 kWe KRUSTY demonstration to a flight‑qualified 5 kWe surface power unit and ultimately to the 1 MWe NEP reactor. Each of these advancements is individually credible; their simultaneous development within the 2026–2043 timeframe proposed in the V&V programme (Section 11.2) represents an ambitious but achievable schedule given adequate investment.

### 13.3.2 SANS Model Completeness

The crew physiological digital twin captures ICP‑driven SANS risk, which accounts for approximately 40–50 % of the total SANS aetiology. Globe flattening, choroidal inflammation, and retinal nerve‑fibre‑layer thickening proceed through pathways not modelled by the two‑compartment ICP formulation. Future work should extend the SANS model to incorporate mechanical modelling of the optic nerve sheath, computational fluid dynamics of the choroidal circulation, and biochemical markers of inflammatory response. The in‑flight OCT monitoring protocol described in Section 6.3.2 provides an empirical safety net while these model improvements are developed.

### 13.3.3 Swarm Federated Learning at Scale

The Byzantine‑robust federated learning theorems proved in Chapter 5 assume \(N = 5\) vehicles with at most one Byzantine node. Extension to larger swarms (\(N = 10\)–\(20\)) with higher Byzantine fractions, more complex network topologies beyond the complete graph, and heterogeneous vehicle capabilities (mix of crewed and uncrewed vehicles, different sensor suites) requires generalisation of the CWTM error bound and empirical validation on larger‑scale testbeds.

### 13.3.4 Long‑Duration Surface ISRU Validation

The ISRU performance estimates are derived from orbital spectroscopy and short‑duration rover soil‑mechanics measurements. The MOXIE experiment provided 16 operational runs over one Mars year; a full‑scale ISRU plant must operate continuously for five years. The effects of multi‑year dust accumulation on solar arrays, regolith‑handling mechanism wear, and SOEC stack degradation at the 950 kg scale remain incompletely characterised. A subscale ISRU demonstrator mission, launched during the 2031 or 2033 opportunity, would provide the necessary long‑duration validation data.

### 13.3.5 Formal Verification of Learning‑Enabled Components

The vCBF safety filter is formally verified by the dReal SMT solver. However, the RL policy itself, the LSTM autoencoder, the GP regression model, and the LLM playbook are learning‑enabled components whose behaviour cannot be exhaustively verified by current formal methods. The architecture mitigates this limitation through the safety filter: even if the RL policy produces an unsafe action, the vCBF QP projects it onto the safe control set before execution. This “shield” architecture provides a practical path to certification of learning‑enabled systems in safety‑critical applications, but ongoing research in formal verification of neural networks — particularly the development of SMT‑based verifiers for transformer architectures — will further strengthen the safety case.

