NASA’s Moon to Mars Architecture defines the capabilities needed for long‑term, human‑led scientific discovery in deep space. The architecture identifies transportation and habitation as the two foundational pillars: creating the systems necessary for humans to travel to the Moon and Mars, live and work there, and return to Earth safely. The STARSHIP‑X architecture presented in this dissertation constitutes a concrete engineering instantiation of that vision, providing the mathematical, physical, and computational specificity required to transition from architectural concept to flight hardware.

The timeline is aggressive but not fanciful. The component technologies — LLM‑based reasoning, RL with formal safety filters, neuromorphic computing, nuclear electric propulsion, HIAD aeroshells, solid‑oxide electrolysis, and federated learning — are all in active development as of 2026. The GUIDE framework will be presented at CVPR 2026. The ASTREA agentic system has demonstrated LLM‑guided supervision on flight‑heritage hardware aboard the ISS. The 120 kW MPD thruster has fired at JPL. The LOFTID HIAD has flown and been recovered. MOXIE has produced oxygen on Mars.

What remains is integration. The STARSHIP‑X architecture provides the blueprint for that integration: not merely a collection of advanced technologies, but a coherent cyber‑physical ecosystem in which each subsystem reinforces the others, where formal mathematics guarantees safety, where the vehicle learns and adapts without human intervention, and where the crew is supported — not replaced — by the autonomy that surrounds them.

The first boot print on Mars will be made by a human. The vehicle that carries that human there, keeps them alive, and brings them home will be a system like STARSHIP‑X: mathematically precise, physically robust, and alive with intelligence.

