# APPENDIX D — KALMAN FILTERING AND STATE ESTIMATION

## D.1 Kalman Filter Derivation

Consider the discrete‑time linear system
\[
\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}_k + \mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}_k + \mathbf{w}_k, \quad \mathbf{w}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{Q}),
\]
\[
\mathbf{y}_k = \mathbf{C}\mathbf{x}_k + \mathbf{v}_k, \quad \mathbf{v}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{R}),
\]
with \(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{v}\) independent. The Kalman filter computes the MMSE estimate \(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k} = \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}_k \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:k}]\).

**Prior:** \(p(\mathbf{x}_k \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:k-1}) = \mathcal{N}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1}, \mathbf{P}_{k|k-1})\).  
**Likelihood:** \(p(\mathbf{y}_k \mid \mathbf{x}_k) = \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{x}_k, \mathbf{R})\).  
**Posterior:** Multiplying the two Gaussians and completing the square yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{S}_k &= \mathbf{C}\mathbf{P}_{k|k-1}\mathbf{C}^{\!\top} + \mathbf{R}, \\
\mathbf{K}_k &= \mathbf{P}_{k|k-1}\mathbf{C}^{\!\top} \mathbf{S}_k^{-1}, \\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k} &= \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1} + \mathbf{K}_k (\mathbf{y}_k - \mathbf{C}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1}), \\
\mathbf{P}_{k|k} &= (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_k \mathbf{C}) \mathbf{P}_{k|k-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

**Gain optimality:** Minimising \(\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{P}_{k|k})\) gives \(\mathbf{K}_k\) as above. The derivation follows from setting the derivative of the trace with respect to \(\mathbf{K}\) to zero.

**Time update:**
\[
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1|k} = \mathbf{A}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k} + \mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}_k, \qquad
\mathbf{P}_{k+1|k} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{P}_{k|k}\mathbf{A}^{\!\top} + \mathbf{Q}.
\]
Under observability and controllability, \(\mathbf{P}_{k|k}\) converges to the unique positive‑definite solution of the discrete algebraic Riccati equation (DARE), allowing a steady‑state gain \(\mathbf{K}_\infty\) to be pre‑computed.

## D.2 Normalised Innovation Squared (NIS) Test

The innovation \(\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k = \mathbf{y}_k - \mathbf{C}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1}\) is zero‑mean with covariance \(\mathbf{S}_k\) under the nominal model. The NIS is
\[
\text{NIS}_k = \tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k^{\!\top} \mathbf{S}_k^{-1} \tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k.
\]

**Theorem D.1 (NIS Distribution).** Under \(\mathcal{H}_0\) (no fault), \(\text{NIS}_k \sim \chi^2(n_y)\) with \(n_y = \dim(\mathbf{y})\).  
*Proof.* \(\mathbf{S}_k^{-1/2}\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})\); the NIS is the sum of \(n_y\) independent squared standard normals. ∎

A point‑wise fault is declared if \(\text{NIS}_k > \chi^2_{n_y,1-\alpha}\). To suppress false alarms from multiple tests, the CUSUM algorithm is used:
\[
g_k = \max(0, g_{k-1} + \text{NIS}_k - \nu),
\]
with \(\nu = \chi^2_{n_y,0.5}\) (median). A fault is declared when \(g_k > h\). The CUSUM test minimises the expected detection delay for a given mean time between false alarms (Lorden optimality).

## D.3 Reduced‑Order Model via POD

Given a snapshot matrix \(\mathbf{X} = [\mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_{N_s}] \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{full}} \times N_s}\) from FEM simulations, compute the SVD \(\mathbf{X} = \mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\!\top}\). The first \(r\) columns of \(\mathbf{U}\), denoted \(\boldsymbol{\Phi}\), form the POD basis. The retained energy fraction is \(\eta_r = (\sum_{i=1}^r \sigma_i^2) / (\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{full}}} \sigma_i^2) \ge 0.999\). The ROM matrices are obtained by Galerkin projection:
\[
\mathbf{A}_{\text{ROM}} = \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{\!\top}\mathbf{A}_{\text{FEM}}\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \quad
\mathbf{B}_{\text{ROM}} = \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{\!\top}\mathbf{B}_{\text{FEM}}, \quad
\mathbf{C}_{\text{ROM}} = \mathbf{C}_{\text{FEM}}\boldsymbol{\Phi}.
\]

