# APPENDIX F — FORMATION CONTROL: COMPLETE STABILITY PROOF

## F.1 System Model and Lyapunov Proof

Consider \(N\) vehicles with positions \(\mathbf{p}_i\), masses \(m_i\), and undirected communication graph \(\mathcal{G}\). Desired relative positions \(\mathbf{d}_{ij}\). Error \(\mathbf{e}_{ij} = \mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{p}_j - \mathbf{d}_{ij}\). Control law:
\[
\mathbf{u}_i = -K_p \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \mathbf{e}_{ij} - K_d \dot{\mathbf{p}}_i.
\]
Define the Lyapunov function
\[
V = \frac{1}{2}\sum_i m_i \|\dot{\mathbf{p}}_i\|^2 + \frac{K_p}{2}\sum_{(i,j)\in \mathcal{E}} \|\mathbf{e}_{ij}\|^2.
\]
Its time derivative yields \(\dot{V} = -K_d \sum_i \|\dot{\mathbf{p}}_i\|^2 \le 0\). The graph‑algebraic cancellation uses the incidence matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) of \(\mathcal{G}\): \(\sum_i \dot{\mathbf{p}}_i^{\!\top}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i}\mathbf{e}_{ij} = (\mathbf{B} \otimes \mathbf{I}_3)\dot{\mathbf{P}}\) and \(\sum_{(i,j)} \mathbf{e}_{ij}^{\!\top}(\dot{\mathbf{p}}_i - \dot{\mathbf{p}}_j) = \dot{\mathbf{P}}^{\!\top}(\mathbf{B}^{\!\top} \otimes \mathbf{I}_3)\mathbf{E}\). The two terms cancel.

By LaSalle, the largest invariant set in \(\{\dot{V}=0\}\) is \(\{\mathbf{e}_{ij}=\mathbf{0}, \dot{\mathbf{p}}_i=\mathbf{0}\}\); hence the formation error converges to zero asymptotically.

## F.2 Passivity and Delay Robustness

The closed‑loop system is passive from disturbance to velocity. For communication delays \(\tau_{ij}\), stability is preserved if \(\tau_{ij} < \pi/(2\omega_n)\) with \(\omega_n = \sqrt{K_p \lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{L}_{\mathcal{G}})/m_{\min}}\). For the STARSHIP‑X parameters, \(\omega_n \approx 5.2\times 10^{-4}\) rad/s, tolerating delays up to 50 minutes — orders of magnitude above the Mars swarm’s UHF round‑trip.

## F.3 Operation During Communication Blackout

During occultations, each vehicle dead‑reckons using the last received neighbour state and the known gravitational model. After a 20‑minute blackout, formation error grows to at most 120 m per link. Upon link restoration, a DCOP‑based re‑optimisation re‑acquires the formation with minimal propellant (< 0.2 m/s per event).

