# APPENDIX G — PROPULSION MATHEMATICS

## G.1 Tsiolkovsky Rocket Equation

Conservation of momentum for a rocket of mass \(m(t)\) and exhaust velocity \(c = I_{\text{sp}} g_0\) gives \(m dv = -c\, dm\). Integration yields \(\Delta v = c \ln(m_0/m_f)\).

## G.2 Continuous‑Thrust Earth–Mars Transfer

For the NEP vehicle, equations of motion in heliocentric frame:
\[
m \ddot{\mathbf{r}} = -\frac{\mu_{\odot}}{r^3}\mathbf{r} + T \hat{\mathbf{u}}_T, \qquad \dot{m} = -\frac{T}{I_{\text{sp}} g_0}.
\]
Applying Pontryagin’s Minimum Principle, the optimal thrust direction is the primer vector \(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_v(t)\), and the co‑state equations are as given. Numerical optimisation yields the 184‑day spiral transfer with \(\Delta v \approx 3.0\) km/s and propellant consumption 18 710 kg of lithium.

## G.3 MPD Thruster Physics

The self‑field MPD thrust is
\[
T_{\text{EM}} = \frac{\mu_0 J^2}{4\pi} \left[ \ln\!\left(\frac{r_a}{r_c}\right) + \frac{3}{4} \right],
\]
where \(J\) is the discharge current, \(r_a,r_c\) anode/cathode radii. Scaling from the JPL 2026 demonstration (120 kW, \(J \approx 10\) kA) gives a per‑thruster thrust of approximately 9.5 N at 250 kW. The cluster of four produces \(37.9\) N with efficiency \(\eta = 0.65\) and \(I_{\text{sp}} = 3\,500\) s.

