# APPENDIX H — THERMAL MATHEMATICS

## H.1 Topology Optimisation (SIMP)

The TPS panel is discretised into \(n_e\) elements with pseudo‑density \(\rho_e\). The stiffness matrix is \(\mathbf{K}(\boldsymbol{\rho}) = \sum \rho_e^p \mathbf{K}_e^0\) with \(p=3\). The compliance minimisation problem:
\[
\min_{\boldsymbol{\rho}} \; \mathbf{f}^{\!\top}\mathbf{u} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbf{K}\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{f},\; \frac{1}{V}\int \rho \, dV \le V_f,\; \nu_{\text{eff}} \le -0.3.
\]
Sensitivities are computed via the adjoint method: \(\partial c/\partial \rho_e = -p \rho_e^{p-1} \mathbf{u}_e^{\!\top} \mathbf{K}_e^0 \mathbf{u}_e\). The auxetic constraint uses homogenisation. The Method of Moving Asymptotes (MMA) is used for the optimisation.

## H.2 Numerical Homogenisation

Effective conductivity tensor: solve \(\nabla \cdot (k(\mathbf{y})(\nabla T_i + \mathbf{e}_i)) = 0\) on unit cell with periodic boundaries. Then
\[
k_{\text{eff},ii} = \frac{1}{|Y|} \int_Y k(\mathbf{y})\left( \frac{\partial T_i}{\partial y_i} + 1 \right) dY.
\]
The design achieves \(k_{\text{eff,normal}} = 0.8\) W/m·K and \(k_{\text{eff,tangential}} = 15\) W/m·K.

## H.3 MPC Transpiration Cooling QP

ROM thermal dynamics: \(\mathbf{T}_{k+1} = \mathbf{A}_d \mathbf{T}_k + \mathbf{B}_{d,q} q_k + \mathbf{B}_{d,c} \dot{m}_c''(k)\). Over horizon \(N_p\), the temperature at step \(k\) is linear in the control sequence \(\mathbf{u} = [\dot{m}_c''(0),\dots,\dot{m}_c''(N_p-1)]^{\!\top}\):
\[
\mathbf{C}_{\max}\mathbf{T}_k = \mathbf{c}_k + \mathbf{h}_k^{\!\top}\mathbf{u}.
\]
The MPC problem becomes the strictly convex QP
\[
\min_{\mathbf{u}} \mathbf{u}^{\!\top}\mathbf{u}\,\Delta t \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbf{H}\mathbf{u} \le \mathbf{b},\; \mathbf{0} \le \mathbf{u} \le \dot{m}_{c,\max}''\mathbf{1}.
\]
The matrices \(\mathbf{H}\) and \(\mathbf{b}\) encode the \(N_p\) temperature constraints. Solved with warm‑started active‑set in \(< 5\) ms.

# APPENDIX I — ISRU ENGINEERING CALCULATIONS

## I.1 Solid‑Oxide Electrolysis — Electrochemical Model

The Mars atmospheric CO₂ is electrolysed in a solid‑oxide electrolysis cell (SOEC) according to the overall reaction  

\[
2\text{CO}_2 \longrightarrow 2\text{CO} + \text{O}_2.
\]

At the cathode, CO₂ is reduced by electrons supplied from the external circuit, releasing oxide ions:

\[
\text{CO}_2 + 2e^- \longrightarrow \text{CO} + \text{O}^{2-}.
\]

The oxide ions migrate through the oxygen‑ion‑conducting ceramic electrolyte (typically yttria‑stabilised zirconia, YSZ) to the anode, where they are oxidised:

\[
2\text{O}^{2-} \longrightarrow \text{O}_2 + 4e^-.
\]

The thermodynamic minimum voltage required to drive the reaction is given by the Nernst equation:

\[
V_{\text{Nernst}} = \frac{\Delta G_{\text{rxn}}}{n_e F},
\]

where \(\Delta G_{\text{rxn}}\) is the Gibbs free energy change of the reaction, \(n_e = 4\) is the number of electrons transferred per molecule of O₂ produced (or 2 per CO₂ molecule reduced), and \(F = 96\,485\ \text{C mol}^{-1}\) is Faraday’s constant. At the MOXIE operating temperature of approximately \(800^\circ\text{C} = 1073\ \text{K}\), \(\Delta G_{\text{rxn}} \approx 395\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}\) (per mole of O₂), giving  

\[
V_{\text{Nernst}} = \frac{395\,000}{2 \times 96\,485} \approx 2.05\ \text{V per cell}.
\]

Practical cells operate above the Nernst voltage due to ohmic losses in the electrolyte, activation overpotentials at the electrodes, and concentration polarisation. MOXIE flight data indicate an operating voltage \(V_{\text{op}} \approx 2.5\) V per cell at a current of \(I = 2\) A, consistent with the stack resistance of approximately \(0.225\ \Omega\) per cell. The oxygen production rate for a stack of \(N_{\text{cells}}\) series‑connected cells is

\[
\dot{m}_{\text{O}_2} = \frac{I \cdot N_{\text{cells}} \cdot M_{\text{O}_2}}{n_e F},
\]

where \(M_{\text{O}_2} = 32\ \text{g mol}^{-1}\). For the Phase 1 plant at 20 kW electrical input, \(V_{\text{op}} = 2.5\) V, and \(N_{\text{cells}} = 4\,000\):

\[
I = \frac{P}{V_{\text{op}} N_{\text{cells}}} = \frac{20\,000}{2.5 \times 4\,000} = 2\ \text{A},
\]

\[
\dot{m}_{\text{O}_2,\text{ideal}} = \frac{2 \times 4\,000 \times 32}{2 \times 96\,485} \approx 1.327\ \text{g s}^{-1} \approx 4\,777\ \text{g h}^{-1}.
\]

Accounting for a Faradaic efficiency of 80 % (some current drives parasitic reactions or is lost to electronic conduction in the electrolyte), the practical production rate is  

\[
\dot{m}_{\text{O}_2,\text{practical}} = 0.80 \times 4\,777 \approx 3\,822\ \text{g h}^{-1}.
\]

A conservative operational rating of 600 g h⁻¹, as used in the main text, corresponds to approximately 16 % of this ideal capacity, providing ample margin for stack degradation, dust‑storm power curtailment, and seasonal duty‑cycle losses over a five‑year plant life.

