## I.2 Regolith Excavation Power Budget

The power required to excavate Martian regolith is dominated by the bucket‑wheel drive and the conveyance system. For a single IPEx‑class rover of mass 800 kg operating in the Martian gravity field (\(g_{\text{Mars}} = 3.72\ \text{m s}^{-2}\)), the traction force is governed by the Coulomb failure criterion for granular materials:

\[
F_t = c A + W \tan\phi,
\]

where \(c\) is the regolith cohesion, \(A\) is the bucket‑wheel contact area, \(W = m g_{\text{Mars}}\) is the normal load, and \(\phi\) is the internal friction angle.

InSight HP³ mole penetration experiments constrain the duricrust cohesion to 2–15 kPa; scoop‑based measurements yield \(c \approx 5.8\ \text{kPa}\). Adopting conservative values for loose regolith below the duricrust, \(c = 1.5\ \text{kPa}\), \(A = 0.08\ \text{m}^2\), \(W = 800 \times 3.72 = 2\,976\ \text{N}\), and \(\phi = 35^\circ\) (from Viking and Curiosity soil‑mechanics data):

\[
F_t = 1\,500 \times 0.08 + 2\,976 \times \tan 35^\circ \approx 120 + 2\,084 = 2\,204\ \text{N}.
\]

The excavation power, at a bucket tangential velocity \(v_{\text{exc}} = 0.5\ \text{m s}^{-1}\) and drive efficiency \(\eta_{\text{drive}} = 0.85\), is

\[
P_{\text{exc}} = \frac{F_t \cdot v_{\text{exc}}}{\eta_{\text{drive}}} = \frac{2\,204 \times 0.5}{0.85} \approx 1\,297\ \text{W} \approx 1.3\ \text{kW}.
\]

For a fleet of three rovers operating simultaneously, the total excavation power is \(3 \times 1.3 = 3.9\ \text{kW}\). The regolith processing plant — which includes crushing, screening, and heating for water extraction — requires an additional 36 kW, bringing the total Phase 2 ISRU power to approximately 40 kW electrical. This load is serviced by a dedicated 40 kWe Kilopower‑class fission reactor.

---

## I.3 Water Extraction Thermodynamics

Water is extracted from hydrated regolith by heating the bulk material to the sublimation point and condensing the evolved vapour. For regolith with a water‑ice content of 3 wt %, producing 1 kg of liquid water requires processing \(m_{\text{reg}} = 1 / 0.03 \approx 33.3\ \text{kg}\) of regolith. The thermal energy required comprises the sensible heat to raise the regolith from the mean surface temperature to the sublimation point, plus the latent heat of phase change:

\[
Q_{\text{ext}} = m_{\text{reg}} c_p (T_{\text{sub}} - T_{\text{amb}}) + m_{\text{water}} \bigl(L_{\text{fus}} + L_{\text{vap}}\bigr).
\]

For basaltic regolith, \(c_p \approx 0.84\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}\). The mean ambient surface temperature is taken as \(T_{\text{amb}} = 213\ \text{K}\) (\(-60^\circ\text{C}\)). Sublimation of ice directly to vapour (bypassing the liquid phase at the low Martian surface pressure) occurs at \(T_{\text{sub}} \approx 273\ \text{K}\); the combined latent heat of fusion and vaporisation is \(L_{\text{fus}} + L_{\text{vap}} \approx 334 + 2\,501 = 2\,835\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}\). Thus

\[
\begin{aligned}
Q_{\text{ext}} &= 33.3 \times 0.84 \times (273 - 213) + 1 \times 2\,835 \\
&\approx 33.3 \times 0.84 \times 60 + 2\,835 \\
&\approx 1\,678 + 2\,835 = 4\,513\ \text{kJ kg}^{-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

For a daily production target of 500 kg of water per sol (86 400 s), the continuous thermal power requirement is

\[
P_{\text{thermal}} = \frac{4\,513 \times 500}{86\,400} \approx 26.1\ \text{kW}.
\]

With electrical heating at 95 % efficiency, the electrical demand is \(26.1 / 0.95 \approx 27.5\ \text{kW}\), which fits within the 40 kWe reactor allocation. Solar‑thermal pre‑heating of the regolith feedstock during daylight hours further reduces the electrical load, providing operational margin.

